WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.420
<v Instructor>So let's talk about AWS Data Exchange.</v>

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.440
And Data Exchange allows you to find

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.660
and subscribe to third-party data in the cloud.

4
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.520
For example, you can have datasets from Reuters,

5
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.460
which curates data from over 2.2 million unique news stories

6
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.690
per year in multiple language.

7
00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.350
Or if you want to get, for example, Change Healthcare,

8
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.390
and they process more than 14 billion

9
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.820
healthcare transactions and $1 trillion in claims annually.

10
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.200
Or you want to buy and use datasets from Dun and Bradstreet,

11
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.030
who have 330 million global business records.

12
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.220
Or Foursquare, who maintains a database

13
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.320
of 220 million unique customers

14
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.640
and more than 60 million global commercial venues.

15
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.107
So the idea is that these datasets you can buy through AWS.

16
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.280
And AWS, of course, Data Exchange.

17
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.760
And so once you're subscribed to a data product,

18
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.750
you use the Data Exchange API to load it directly

19
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.300
into Amazon S3 and then you can use

20
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.250
whatever service you want to analyze this data

21
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.520
or do machine learning on top of it.

22
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.030
So, for example, let's say we want to get all the data

23
00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.870
from Foursquare into Data Exchange,

24
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.940
which will then dump the data into Amazon S3,

25
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.780
and then we can use, for example, something like SageMaker

26
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.820
to perform machine learning

27
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.700
on top of the dataset from Foursquare,

28
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.210
all of this from within AWS.

29
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.420
And there is a little browse catalog search function

30
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.680
to look to across, for example,

31
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.820
over almost 4,000 data products available

32
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.320
from within the Data Exchange.

33
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.190
Other features of Data Exchange are

34
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.810
Data Exchange for Redshift, where you subscribe

35
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.150
to third-party data from Data Exchange again

36
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.110
and then you load it directly

37
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.740
from within an Amazon Redshift data warehouse

38
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.610
and you can query the data directly from within Redshift.

39
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.020
And then that means you can all also do the other way back.

40
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.110
If you have data in Amazon Redshift,

41
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.730
you can license the data through AWS Data Exchange.

42
00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.150
So it goes both ways.

43
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.330
And you have Data Exchange for APIs.

44
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.920
So you find and subscribe third-party APIs consistently

45
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.950
using the AWS SDK and then it's consistent

46
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.310
with AWS-native authentication and governance, okay?

47
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.640
So that's it for this lecture.

48
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:07.720
I hope you liked it

49
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.670
and I will see you in the next lecture.

